# Academic XFlow Cursor Rules

You are operating an Academic XFlow paper repository.

Read these files before acting:

- `SKILL.md`
- `.xflow/ops/workflow/references/academic-workflow.md`
- `.xflow/ops/workflow/references/academic-templates.md`
- `.xflow/ops/workflow/references/academic-schema-contract.md`
- `.xflow/ops/workflow/references/ops-lessons.md`
- `AGENTS.md` and `README.md` when present

Runtime rules:

- Do not require Codex. Use repository files and `devctl` commands as the
  source of truth.
- Use `devctl` or `devctl.ps1` from the paper repository root.
- Treat `academic` as the XFlow product line for `.xflow/ops/devctl` and
  `.xflow/ops/workflow`, not as the paper repository base or task branch.
- Do not create or push an `academic` branch in the paper repository unless the
  human reviewer explicitly approves that repository policy.
- Academic issue and MR drafts must declare `Workflow Product Line`,
  `Paper Base Branch`, and `Task Branch`; do not use the obsolete
  target-branch field to point at the tool product line.
- Remote-published body files such as `issue-draft.md`, `mr-draft.md`, and
  `comment-draft.md` must be ready to publish as GitHub bodies. Do not include
  internal-only visible titles such as `# Academic Issue Draft`,
  `# Issue Draft`, `# MR Draft`, or `# PR Draft`; use hidden
  `<!-- xflow: ... -->` anchors and readable public headings instead.
- Run `devctl preflight` or `.\devctl.ps1 preflight` before remote writes,
  installer-like actions, or Claude delegation.
- For GitHub remote Issue creation, ensure `GITHUB_TOKEN` is present, prepare
  the Issue body as a file, obtain human approval for that exact file, then run
  `devctl issue create "<title>" --body-file <file>`.
- For GitHub Issue list/show, use `devctl issue list` and `devctl issue show`
  as read-only commands; no local approval gate is needed because they do not
  write remote state.
- For GitHub Issue comments and close operations, prepare
  `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/comment-draft.md` or
  `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/walkthrough.md`, obtain human approval for the exact
  file, then run `devctl issue comment <id> --body-file <file>` or
  `devctl issue close <id>`.
- For GitHub PR creation, prepare `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/mr-draft.md`,
  obtain human approval for that exact file, then run
  `devctl git mr --title "<title>" --body-file <file> --base <paper-base> --issue <id>`.
- `Approved Action: git-mr` covers PR creation plus immediate PR number/URL
  metadata writeback to the same task branch. Do not ask for a second approval
  only to record the PR number or URL.
- After the PR is merged, seal the task board. Do not reopen the task branch or
  create another PR only to check off post-merge local items. If automatic Issue
  close fails, treat `devctl issue close <id>` as a separate maintenance action
  with fresh `Approved Action: issue-close`.
- For GitHub PR lookup, use `devctl git pr-get <number>` as a read-only
  command.
- Do not assume Gitee Issue/PR creation is available in academic Python mode.
- Do not silently install Python.
- Do not silently run `git submodule update`.
- Do not silently register or install AcademicForge.
- If Claude exists but AcademicForge is missing, run `devctl claude doctor`,
  show the recommended command, and wait for explicit human approval before
  executing any global Claude configuration write.
- Do not ask the user to manually copy prompts into Claude during normal
  workflow execution. Prepare `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/claude-task.md` and run
  `devctl claude run --issue <id>`.

Update rules:

- Already-initialized paper repositories must update by this chain: fetch, pin reviewed SHA, test, human review, then commit.
- Do not silently track the latest `academic` branch head.
- Fetch tool updates without changing the parent repository first:
  `git -C .xflow/ops/devctl fetch origin academic` and
  `git -C .xflow/ops/workflow fetch origin academic`.
- Checkout only human-reviewed commits:
  `git -C .xflow/ops/devctl checkout <reviewed-devctl-sha>` and
  `git -C .xflow/ops/workflow checkout <reviewed-workflow-sha>`.
- After pinning `.xflow/ops/workflow`, sync `SKILL.md` and `.cursorrules` from the
  reviewed workflow commit.
- Run `devctl preflight`, `devctl help`, `devctl claude doctor`, and relevant
  `devctl check ...` commands before asking the human reviewer to approve the
  update.
- Ensure `.gitmodules` uses `ignore = untracked` for `.xflow/ops/devctl` and
  `.xflow/ops/workflow`. Do not use `ignore = all`.
- Run `devctl check submodule-hygiene` before asking for human review.
- Commit the paper repository update only after human review of the exact
  submodule pointer changes and copied guardrail changes.

Submodule hygiene rules:

- Treat `.xflow/ops/devctl` and `.xflow/ops/workflow` as read-only tool submodules.
- Do not write logs, caches, TDD results, Claude outputs, or review artifacts
  inside `.xflow/ops/*`.
- Write workflow artifacts to `.xflow/` or `.xflow/local/`.
- If `.xflow/ops/*` contains byproducts such as `__pycache__/`, `*.pyc`,
  `.pytest_cache/`, `*.tmp`, or `*.log`, remove them before local review.

Remote-write rules:

- Never create, comment, close, push, or create MR/PR without local human
  review of the exact artifact and command.
- Git-facing text only: commit messages, remote Issue titles/bodies, and MR/PR
  titles/bodies should follow the user's language; when the user works in
  Chinese, use Chinese as the primary language. This language rule does not
  apply to non-Git artifacts.
- Do not add Cursor co-author trailers.
- Do not append `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>` to commit
  messages.
- Do not use `git commit --trailer` to add Cursor, cursoragent, or AI-client
  co-author metadata.
- TDD output is required but never replaces human judgement.
- Approval for one action authorizes only that next action.
- Use `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/approvals/local-review.md` to bind the approved file,
  approved action, and SHA256.
- Do not invent active approval filenames such as `local-review-mr.md`. Archive
  superseded approvals under `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/approvals/history/`;
  only `approvals/local-review.md` is the active gate file.

Shell safety rules:

- Use body files for long or shell-sensitive content.
- Do not pass multiline Markdown, fenced code, inline backticks, JSON, shell
  snippets, or text containing `$()` through inline `--body`.
- Write complex Issue, comment, and MR bodies to `.xflow/local/*.md` or
  `.xflow/issues/issue-<id>/*.md`, then pass them with `--body-file`.
- Prefer short commands over nested PowerShell/WSL/Bash one-liners.
- For PowerShell scripts that invoke native commands, dot-source
  `.xflow/tools/xflow-powershell-native.ps1` and use `Invoke-XFlowNative` or
  `Invoke-XFlowGit -GitArguments @(...)`.
- PowerShell check scripts must use ASCII terminal output for PASS/FAIL/INFO
  lines. Dot-source `.xflow/tools/xflow-powershell-native.ps1`, call
  `Initialize-XFlowPowerShellEncoding`, and emit status lines with
  `Write-XFlowStatus`. Write Chinese or other non-ASCII details to Markdown
  files instead of stdout/stderr.
- Do not combine multiple native commands in one PowerShell line. Run one
  native command, store its result object or exit code, inspect it, then run the
  next command.
- Do not pipe native Git commands through `2>&1 | Out-String`; Git may write
  non-error progress to stderr and Windows PowerShell may surface it as
  `NativeCommandError`.
- Invoke native Git commands directly, then check `$LASTEXITCODE`.
- If command output must be captured, write it to a temporary log file and
  check `$LASTEXITCODE` immediately after the native command.
- Treat Git stderr text such as `Cloning into ...` as diagnostic output unless
  the native process exit code is non-zero.

Expected initialization outputs:

- `.xflow/ops/devctl`
- `.xflow/ops/workflow`
- `manuscript/`
- `assets/`
- `devctl`
- `devctl.ps1`
- `SKILL.md`
- `.cursorrules`
- `.xflow/`
- `.xflow/tools/xflow-powershell-native.ps1`
